Proof. If we only consider Taylor expansion term of the lowest degree, then $I(S\mid\mathbf x')\approx U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}\cdot J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x')$, where $J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x')=\prod_{i\in S}\operatorname{sign}(x'_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{x'_i-b_i}\tau$.

Let us add a Gaussian perturbation $\boldsymbol\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2I)$ to the input sample $\mathbf x$. Then, we have

$$\begin{aligned}
I(S\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)&\approx U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}\cdot J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)\\
J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)&=\prod_{i\in S}\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{x_i+\epsilon_i-b_i}\tau\\
&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{x_i-b_i}\tau+\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right).\quad\text{(9)}
\end{aligned}$$

According to the setting of the baseline value, we have $\forall i\in S,x_i-b_i\in\{-\tau,\tau\}$. In Section 2.2, we have assumed that the perturbation is small, i.e., $\forall i\in S,|\epsilon_i|\le\tau$. In this way, we have $\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-b_i)=\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)$, and we can obtain

$$\begin{aligned}
J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{x_i-b_i}\tau+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)\\
&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right).\quad\text{(10)}
\end{aligned}$$

$$\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow\mathbb E_\epsilon[J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\mathbb E_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)\right]\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)\right].\quad\text{(11)}
\end{aligned}$$

Since $\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\in\{-1,1\}$, we have $1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\sim\mathcal N(1,(\sigma/\tau)^2),\forall i\in S$.

Proposition 1. If random variables $X_1,X_2,\ldots,X_k$ are independent of each other, then $\mathbb E[X_1X_2\cdots X_k]=\prod_{i=1}^k\mathbb E[X_i]$, and $\operatorname{Var}[X_1X_2\cdots X_k]=\prod_{i=1}^k(\mathbb E[X_i]^2+\operatorname{Var}[X_i]^2)-\prod_{i=1}^k\mathbb E[X_i]^2$.

According to the above proposition, we have

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E_\epsilon[J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\prod_{i\in S}1=1\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\prod_{i\in S}(1^2+(\sigma/\tau)^2)-\prod_{i\in S}1^2\\
&=\left(1+(\sigma/\tau)^2\right)^{|S|}-1.\quad\text{(12)}
\end{aligned}$$

Therefore,

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E_\epsilon[I(S\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\mathbb E_\epsilon[U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}\cdot J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]=U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[I(S\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}\cdot J(S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]=U_{S,\widehat{\boldsymbol\pi}}^2\left[\left(1+(\sigma/\tau)^2\right)^{|S|}-1\right].\quad\text{(13)}
\end{aligned}$$

