According to Theorem 2, given an arbitrary input sample $\mathbf x'$, we have

$$J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x')=\prod_{i\in S}\left(\operatorname{sign}(x'_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{x'_i-b_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}.\qquad\text{(14)}$$

Let us add a Gaussian perturbation $\boldsymbol\epsilon\sim\mathcal N(0,\sigma^2I)$ to the input sample $\mathbf x$. In this way, we have

$$\begin{aligned}
J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{x_i+\epsilon_i-b_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\\
&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{x_i-b_i}\tau+\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}.\quad\text{(15)}
\end{aligned}$$

According to the setting of the baseline value, $\forall i\in S,x_i-b_i\in\{-\tau,\tau\}$. We assume that the perturbation is small, i.e., $\forall i\in S,|\epsilon_i|\ll\tau$. In this way, $\operatorname{sign}(x_i+\epsilon_i-b_i)=\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)$, and we can obtain

$$\begin{aligned}
J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{x_i-b_i}\tau+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\\
&=\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}.\quad\text{(16)}
\end{aligned}$$

$$\begin{aligned}
\Rightarrow\mathbb E_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\mathbb E_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right]\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right].\quad\text{(17)}
\end{aligned}$$

Since $\forall i\in S,\epsilon_i$ is independent of each other, according to Proposition 1 and Eq. (17), we have

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\prod_{i\in S}\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}\left[\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right]\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\prod_{i\in S}\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}\left[\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{2\pi_i}\right]-\prod_{i\in S}\left(\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}\left[\left(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right]\right)^2.\quad\text{(18)}
\end{aligned}$$

Since $\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\in\{-1,1\}$, we have $\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}[(1+\operatorname{sign}(x_i-b_i)\cdot\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau)^k]=\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}[(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau)^k],\forall k\in\mathbb N^+$. Therefore, we obtain

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbb E_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\prod_{i\in S}\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}\left[\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right]\\
&=\mathbb E_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right]\\
\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon[J(S,\boldsymbol\pi\mid\mathbf x+\boldsymbol\epsilon)]&=\prod_{i\in S}\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}\left[\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{2\pi_i}\right]-\prod_{i\in S}\left(\mathbb E_{\epsilon_i}\left[\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right]\right)^2\\
&=\operatorname{Var}_\epsilon\left[\prod_{i\in S}\left(1+\frac{\epsilon_i}\tau\right)^{\pi_i}\right].
\end{aligned}$$
